---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import C from "../data/consistency.json";
import CAUSES from "../data/impossible.json";

const SEV = { 1: "Impossible", 2: "Very doubtful", 3: "Worth a look" };
const KIND = {
  "child-after-parent-death": "A child born after the parent died",
  "child-before-parent-birth": "A child born before the parent",
  "parent-too-young": "A parent under thirteen",
  "mother-too-old": "A mother over fifty",
  "died-before-born": "Died before being born",
  "married-before-birth": "Married before being born",
  "married-after-death": "Married after dying",
  "married-as-child": "Married as a child",
  "implausible-age": "Lived past a hundred and five",
  "impossible-generation-gap": "Two generations in too few years",
};
const CAUSE = {
  record:     ["A contradiction in the record", "The dates as written cannot both be true. This is the only kind that is really a finding about the past rather than about the tree."],
  namesake:   ["A wrong link", "Too far apart for any slop in the dates. The join is wrong, not the dates — usually a reused name, or two people merged."],
  generation: ["A generation skipped", "The parent is barely older than the child, because a grandparent has been recorded as a parent."],
  hedged:     ["Not an error at all", "A hedged date (BEF, AFT, Maybe) sitting against a firm one. The hedge is doing its job."],
};
const ORDER = ["record", "namesake", "generation", "hedged"];
const causeRows = (k) => CAUSES.filter((c) => c.cause === k);
const bySev = (n) => C.filter((i) => i.sev === n);
const counts = [1, 2, 3].map((n) => [n, bySev(n).length]);

// grouped, because the interesting unit is a person with several at once
const byPerson = {};
for (const i of C) (byPerson[i.name] ||= { name: i.name, slug: i.slug, rows: [] }).rows.push(i);
const worst = Object.values(byPerson).sort((a, b) => b.rows.length - a.rows.length).slice(0, 6);
const kindCounts = Object.entries(
  C.reduce((a, i) => ((a[i.kind] = (a[i.kind] || 0) + 1), a), {})
).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
---
<Base title="What Cannot Be True"
  eyebrow={`${C.length} contradictions the tree makes with itself`}
  dek="No register was needed for any of these. They are dates in one record that cannot all be true at once — a mother who died before her children were born, a woman married eight years after her own death. Checking a tree against itself is the cheapest research there is, and it had never been done here.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This page exists because of <a href={u("/people/tereza-pecanic")}>Tereza
    Pećanić</a>. She is a direct ancestor, and this archive published her as having
    <strong>died before 1850</strong> — while the same record married her in <strong>1858</strong>
    and gave her children until <strong>1884</strong>. Nothing external was needed to see it. Her
    own page contradicted itself, and it had done so for as long as the site existed.</p>
    <p>So <span class="mono">tools/consistency.py</span> now reads every person in this archive
    against themselves and against their own family: births after deaths, marriages before births,
    parents younger than their children. It finds <strong>{C.length}</strong>.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>By how bad</h3>
        <table class="kv"><tbody>{counts.map(([n, k]) => (
          <tr><th>{SEV[n]}</th><td><strong class="mono">{k}</strong></td></tr>))}</tbody></table>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem"><em>Impossible</em> means the dates are firm and
        still conflict. <em>Very doubtful</em> means one of them is hedged — <span class="mono">ABT</span>,
        <span class="mono">BEF</span> — so the conflict may be an artefact of the hedge.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>By kind</h3>
        <table class="kv"><tbody>{kindCounts.map(([k, n]) => (
          <tr><th>{KIND[k] || k}</th><td><strong class="mono">{n}</strong></td></tr>))}</tbody></table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>Sixty-three rows are not sixty-three mistakes</h2>
    <p class="lede">Every row above is a <em>symptom</em>. One wrong parent link reports once per
    child, so the page was counting its own consequences — and worse, it was listing four quite
    different things as though they were one. Grouping by pivot person and by cause, the
    <strong>{C.length}</strong> rows collapse to <strong>{CAUSES.length}</strong> distinct
    mistakes.</p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Kind of mistake</th><th>Causes</th><th>Rows</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{ORDER.map((k) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{CAUSE[k][0]}</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">{causeRows(k).length}</td>
          <td class="mono dim">{causeRows(k).reduce((a, c) => a + c.n, 0)}</td>
          <td class="dim">{CAUSE[k][1]}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The distinction matters because only one of these four is a
    finding about <em>the past</em>. A wrong link and a skipped generation are findings about
    <em>this tree</em>, and a hedged date conflicting with a firm one is not a finding at all —
    it is the hedge working as intended. Presenting them in one undifferentiated list of
    “contradictions” overstated the first and slandered the last.</p>

    {ORDER.map((k) => (
      <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
        <h3>{CAUSE[k][0]} — {causeRows(k).length}</h3>
        <p class="dek">{CAUSE[k][1]}</p>
        <table class="reg">
          <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Rows</th><th>The contradiction</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{causeRows(k).map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{c.slug
                ? <a href={u("/people/" + c.slug)}>{c.name}</a>
                : <span>{c.name} <span class="dim">— no page</span></span>}</td>
              <td class="mono">{c.n}</td>
              <td class="mono dim" style="font-size:.86em">{c.rows[0]}{c.n > 1 &&
                <span class="dim"> … and {c.n - 1} more like it</span>}</td>
            </tr>))}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>))}
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>They are mostly one mistake, made repeatedly</h2>
    <p class="lede">Sort by person rather than by error and the shape appears at once: a
    <strong>woman attached as mother to a family she cannot have borne</strong>. Not scattered
    slips — the same slip, over and over, and almost always to a woman.</p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Contradictions</th><th>What is wrong</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{worst.map((p) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{p.slug
            ? <a href={u("/people/" + p.slug)}><strong>{p.name}</strong></a>
            : <span><strong>{p.name}</strong> <span class="dim">— no page</span></span>}</td>
          <td class="mono">{p.rows.length}</td>
          <td class="dim">{p.rows[0].msg}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>The clearest case is not on the direct line at all</h3>
      <p><strong>Vladimira Baricević</strong>, born <strong>12 December 1907</strong>, is entered as
      the mother of <strong>ten</strong> Boras children born <strong>1888 to 1911</strong> — nine of
      them before she was born, one of them in the same month. She is not a doubtful reading or a
      loose date. She is <strong>somebody else's daughter married into the wrong generation</strong>,
      and every child hangs off her.</p>
      <p>Which is exactly what happened to <a href={u("/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic")}>Maria
      Catharina Orešković</a> — four children born when she was two, five and six, plus the son born
      the year before her. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Question 4d</a> worked that one out the
      slow way, from three parish registers. This check finds the same shape in seconds, without
      leaving the file.</p>
      <p class="dek">Seventeen of the {C.length} sit on people with pages here. The rest are
      relatives who appear in someone's family list without a page of their own — still worth
      naming, because a wrong join is visible from both ends.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {[1, 2, 3].map((sev) => (
    <section class="blk">
      <div class="lab">{SEV[sev]}</div>
      <h2>{sev === 1 ? "Dates that are firm and still conflict" : sev === 2 ? "Where one date is hedged" : "Unlikely rather than impossible"}</h2>
      {sev === 3 && <p class="lede">A mother of fifty-one and a man of a hundred and eleven are
        both possible, and both are usually a mistake. These were computed from the first day this
        check existed and were <strong>held back from the page</strong> until now, on the thin
        grounds that they were not certain enough — which is the opposite of how the rest of this
        archive works. They are published here with their doubt attached.</p>}
      {sev === 2 && <p class="lede">A <span class="mono">BEF 1850</span> or
        <span class="mono"> ABT 1782</span> is somebody's estimate. These conflicts may dissolve
        if the estimate is simply wrong — which is itself worth knowing, because it means the
        estimate is doing work it cannot support.</p>}
      <table class="reg">
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What kind</th><th>The contradiction</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{bySev(sev).map((i) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{i.slug ? <a href={u("/people/" + i.slug)}>{i.name}</a> : i.name}</td>
            <td class="dim">{KIND[i.kind] || i.kind}</td>
            <td class="mono" style="font-size:.86em">{i.msg}</td>
          </tr>))}</tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The case the first version missed</div>
    <h2>Two years is not enough for two generations</h2>
    <p class="lede">The checks above compare a parent's dates with a child's — which needs
    <em>both</em> to exist. Where the generation in between is undated, a contradiction falls
    straight through. Reading the <a href={u("/papic")}>Karlobag registers</a> turned up exactly
    that, on the direct line.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Ahnentafel 214</th><td><a href={u("/people/caetano-bacchi")}><strong>Caetano
          Bacchi</strong></a>, born <span class="mono">“12 January 1779 (Maybe)”</span> — the
          hedge is the tree's own</td></tr>
        <tr><th>His daughter</th><td><a href={u("/people/oliva-andreana-bacchi")}>Oliva Andreana
          Bacchi</a> — died 15 September 1795, <strong>no birth date at all</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Her daughter</th><td><a href={u("/people/oliva-maria-smojver")}>Oliva Maria
          Smojver</a>, born <strong>11 March 1781</strong> — confirmed in the register</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">A man born in 1779 cannot be grandfather to a girl born in 1781.
    And Oliva Andreana died in 1795, so she was born well before 1781 herself — her father belongs
    somewhere around <strong>1730–1745</strong>. The 1779 baptism is real; it simply records a
    different Caetano Bacchi.</p>
    <p><strong>It takes two more ancestors with it.</strong> <a href={u("/people/angelo-bacchi")}>Angelo
    Bacchi</a> (ahn 428) and <a href={u("/people/catharina")}>Catharina</a> (ahn 429) are in this
    pedigree <em>only</em> because that entry names them as the child's parents. If the baptism is
    not his, they are not his parents — and Catharina is one of the
    <a href={u("/name")}>seven women here with no surname</a>, so nothing else anchors her.</p>
    <p class="dek">The tool now also walks <strong>grandparent → undated child → grandchild</strong>
    and requires at least twenty-four years across two generations. In this archive it fires
    exactly once.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And it caught three people who are not people</h2>
    <p class="lede">Running the check surfaced <strong>four sorting buckets being published as
    ancestors</strong> — <span class="mono">1800-1830 Birth — Selce Papić (Papa)</span> and three
    like it. The build has always excluded research scaffolding, on the stated grounds that
    publishing it "would be a lie"; the filter simply did not recognise a name that opens with a
    year, or one reading <em>Brothers of Casparus (It Seems)</em>.</p>
    <p>They are gone. (The person count quoted here when this was written, 1,086, has since been overtaken: a surname-fold fix brought 214 more people into the archive.) They had
    children attached to them, which is how the check found them: a bucket dated
    <span class="mono">1800-1830</span> cannot be a parent to somebody born in 1824.
    <a href={u("/changed")}>What changed →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026 — worked one at a time</div>
    <h2>The twenty “record” contradictions, gone through</h2>
    <p class="lede">The section above sorts the rows by <em>kind</em>. This one goes through the
    only kind that is a finding about the past, and asks of each what is actually wrong. They
    collapse further — into five situations and a short list of single slips.</p>

    <div class="qa">
      <h3>1. Three parents die in 1845 and have children in 1847</h3>
      <p><a href={u("/people/elias-ilija-zubrinic-xubrinich")}>Elias Žubrinić</a> (d. 1845, child
      born 1847), <strong>Phillipus Žubrinić</strong> (d. 1845, child born 1847) and a
      <strong>Maria</strong> (d. 1845, child born 1847). Three of the same shape in one family in
      one year.</p>
      <p><strong>1845 is the worst year in the whole Žubrinić death record</strong> — four deaths
      out of only fifty-two that carry a year at all. The
      <a href={u("/searched")}>letter to Gospić</a> already asks about it, because Elias and Ana
      Untersinger both died that year leaving seven children, which suggested an epidemic. Either
      reading works from here: an epidemic year whose deaths were recorded and whose surviving
      children were then attached to the wrong couple, or three ordinary mis-joins that happen to
      share a year. <strong>The Otočac death register for the 1840s would separate them and nothing
      in this file will.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>2. The Boras generation, skipped</h3>
      <p>Four rows — <strong>Budimir Boras</strong> (d. 1954, child born 1960),
      <strong>Marica Milković</strong> (b. 1904, child born 1960, age 56),
      <a href={u("/people/joannes-ivan-boras")}>Joannes Ivan Boras</a> (d. 1926, child born 1930)
      and the eight rows on <strong>Vladimira Baricević</strong> — are all one family and all the
      same error. <strong>Vladimira Boras, born 1960, is attached to a couple born in the 1890s and
      1900s</strong>, and Vladimira Baricević, born 1907, to a man born 1855. A generation is
      missing in both directions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>3. One typing slip that produced three impossibilities</h3>
      <p><strong>Georgius Prpić</strong>, entered as born <strong>26 March 1962</strong> to parents
      who died in 1888 and 1891 — reported here as a child born after his father's death, a child
      born after his mother's, and a mother of 139. His own birthplace settles it:
      <span class="mono">Krivi Put 136, Senj, Lika, <strong>Austria-Hungary</strong></span>, a state
      that ceased to exist in 1918, at his parents' own house. Everything agrees with
      <strong>1862</strong>. <a href={u("/changed")}>What changed →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>4. Two that are already open questions</h3>
      <p><a href={u("/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic")}>Maria Catharina Orešković</a>,
      born 1835, as mother of a boy baptised 1834 — this is
      <a href={u("/open-questions")}>question 4d</a>, worked out the slow way from three parish
      registers. And <strong>Michael “Mate” Žubrinić</strong>, dead in 1863 with a son born in 1880,
      which is the <em>one man or two</em> question the
      <a href={u("/searched")}>Gospić letter</a> exists to settle.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>5. And six single slips with nothing behind them</h3>
      <table class="reg">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Antonia Šestan</strong></td><td class="mono dim">born 11 Feb 1847, died 4 Aug 1846</td>
            <td class="dek">Died before born. A one-digit year error, and the archive cannot say which of the two.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Hector Defranceschi</strong></td><td class="mono dim">born 1896, married 1835</td>
            <td class="dek">A marriage attached to the wrong man of the name.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Hellena Bellobalbić</strong></td><td class="mono dim">born 1868, married 1868</td>
            <td class="dek">Married at nought. Almost certainly her parents' marriage year, carried onto her.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Anna</strong></td><td class="mono dim">born 1809, husband born 1807 — child born 1807</td>
            <td class="dek">One of the <a href={u("/name")}>seven women here with no surname</a>, so nothing else anchors her.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Luka Katnić</strong></td><td class="mono dim">died 1755, children born 1757 and 1768</td>
            <td class="dek">Thirteen years apart. The second child cannot be explained by a posthumous birth.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Catharina</strong> / <strong>Matia Sekula</strong></td><td class="mono dim">mothers at 60 and 51</td>
            <td class="dek">Possible, unlikely, and unresolvable without the registers.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem" class="dek"><strong>So of twenty, three are solved, nine are two
    family-sized mis-joins, two are questions already open, and six are single slips that need a
    register.</strong> Not one of them required reading a new document — only reading the ones
    already here against each other.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this does not claim</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>It does not say which date is wrong.</strong> When a mother dies before her child
      is born, either date could be the bad one, and the check has no way to choose. It only says
      they cannot both stand.</li>
      <li><strong>It does not touch the tree.</strong> Nothing here has been edited at source. The
      contradictions are reproduced, not resolved — the same rule the rest of this archive follows.
      <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></li>
      <li><strong>It is scoped to this family.</strong> The export carries six family trees; run
      across all 15,643 people the same check returns <strong>554</strong>. Those belong to the
      other archives, and are their business.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
